{"schema":1,"id":"python-dict","title":"Why does a deleted dictionary slot need a marker?","lang":"en","sources":["https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/v3.12.14/Objects/dictobject.c","https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/v3.12.14/Include/internal/pycore_dict.h","https://just-taking-a-ride.com/inside_python_dict/"],"settings":[{"id":"key","label":"Look up key","default":"8","options":[{"value":"8","label":"8: pear"},{"value":"16","label":"16: plum"}]}],"answer":"An emptied slot would end the search early, so deletion leaves a marker that says keep looking","checks":[{"question":"Keep key 0, but delete key 8 from slot 1. What happens to the lookup of key 16 if slot 1 is cleared, and if it is marked D?","answer":"Clearing slot 1 makes key 16 unreachable: its lookup visits slots 0, 1 and 6, so it stops at 1 before reaching plum. A D in slot 1 keeps the search moving to slot 6. A deletion marker matters in the middle of a probe path too."},{"question":"After deleting key 0 correctly, someone replaces every never-used slot with D. Could a lookup of absent key 24 still use the stopping rule this lesson shows?","answer":"No. It would have no never-used slot at which to stop. D means keep looking, not absent. A real dict must preserve an unused slot or rebuild its table; this fixed-size model refuses a table with no unused stopping slot."}],"cases":[{"values":{"key":"8"},"name":"Look up key: 8: pear","duration":137.333,"paper":"#ffffff","steps":[{"step":"question","title":"Delete key 0. Can Python still find key 8?","line":"Predict: if del d[0] just empties slot 0, what does d[8] give?","caption":"This dict uses an index and a separate entry table: a slot points to the entry holding its key and value. Here slot 1 points to pear’s entry. The – marks a slot that has never been used. Delete key 0 by clearing slot 0 and its entry. Would d[8] still find pear?","start":0,"end":19.9,"hold":0.533,"state":{"dict":{"indices":[0,1,-1,-1,-1,-1,2,-1],"entries":[{"key":0,"value":"appl