 0. –. –. 1. 8. pear. 2. 16. plum. d[24]. Visit: 0 > 1 > 6 > 7. not found. Slot 7 has never been used, so key 24 is absent.","drawing":"┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│         0          1          2          3         4          5          6          7\n│     ┌────────┐┌─────────┐┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐┌─────────┐┌────────┐\n│     │   D    ││    1    ││    –   │ │    –   │ │   –    │ │   –    ││    2    ││    –   │\n│     └────────┘└─────────┘└────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘└─────────┘└────────┘\n│\n│\n│          Entry          Key            Value                            d[24]\n│\n│       ─────────────────────────────────────────\n│                0       –             –                        Visit: 0 > 1 > 6 > 7\n│\n│                1       8              pear\n│                                                                     not found\n│\n│                2       16              plum\n│\n│\n│                       Slot 7 has never been used, so key 24 is absent.\n│\n│"},{"step":"hash","title":"Why slot 0: all three keys start there","line":"An eight-slot index keeps only the hash’s low three bits.","caption":"For these keys, Python uses the integer itself as its hash, so the hashes are 0, 8, 16. With eight slots, Python keeps only the last three bits of the hash, and all three end in 000 in binary, so all three keys start at slot 0. Key 0 was stored first and took it.","start":87.833,"end":108.467,"hold":88.533,"state":{"dict":{"indices":[0,1,-1,-1,-1,-1,2,-1],"entries":[{"key":0,"value":"apple"},{"key":8,"value":"pear"},{"key":16,"value":"plum"}]},"result":{"key":16,"slots":[0,1,6],"found":true,"value":"plum","stopped":"key"},"key":16,"policy":"CPython marker","phase":"hash"},"changed":{"dict":{"before":{"indices":[-2,1,-1,-1,-1,-1,2,-1],"entries":[{"key":null,"value":null},{"key":8,"value":"pear"},{"key":16,"value":"plum"}]},"after":{"in